From f1f372793ed6b3ee097e80521b9d44d78f3f637f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 22:21:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Add OpenSea Drops watcher as the primary mint path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds the SeaDrop/Drops integration alongside the existing on-chain
detector, so mints go through OpenSea's own transaction builder rather
than a probed function signature.

- opensea/client.ts: v2 REST client with X-API-KEY, per-request timeout,
  and 429/5xx retry that honours Retry-After
- opensea/drops.ts: getDrops / getDrop / buildDropMintTransaction, with
  tolerant normalisation of prices (wei strings, decimal ETH, nested
  {wei}/{value}), timestamps (seconds, millis, ISO), and snake vs camel
  case field names. Endpoint paths are isolated in DROP_ENDPOINTS
- drops/parser.ts: stage selection — Ethereum only, Public stages only,
  price ceiling, supply check, maxPerWallet clamp, watch vs mint vs skip
- watcher.ts: two-tier poller, 60s catalog refresh maintaining a
  persistent watchlist plus 2.5s polling only inside a stage's go-live
  window
- dropMinter.ts: re-validates the API-built transaction before signing,
  refusing any non-zero value, and applies a total gas-price ceiling
  that skips retryably rather than terminally
- store: watchlist, minted-by-slug dedupe, and one-attempt-per-stage
  tracking so a reverting drop cannot be retried in a tight loop

An unparseable price is treated as not-free rather than free, so a
payload the normaliser does not understand can never mint by accident.

The run loop now ticks at the fast cadence when the watcher is active,
with each subsystem deciding internally whether it is due, so the
on-chain log sweep stays on its own slower interval.

Tests go from 55 to 96, adding a mock api.opensea.io alongside the
existing mock JSON-RPC node to cover the catalog to mint flow, rate-limit
backoff, dedupe, quantity clamping, the gas cap, and the kill switch.

