-## How it works
+There are **two independent detectors**. The Drops watcher is the primary path;
+the on-chain detector is a superset that catches what OpenSea doesn't index.
+
+## Detector 1: OpenSea Drops watcher (primary)
+
+`src/watcher.ts`, `src/opensea/`, `src/drops/parser.ts`
+
+Uses the official [Drops API](https://docs.opensea.io/docs/mint-from-a-drop) —
+`getDrops` → `getDrop(slug)` → `buildDropMintTransaction` — so mints go through
+OpenSea's own transaction builder rather than a guessed function signature.
+
+Two-tier polling, deliberately not aggressive:
+
+1. **Catalog refresh** every 60s over `upcoming` + `featured`, maintaining a
+   persistent watchlist.
+2. **Active window** only: drops whose public stage opens within ±120s get
+   polled every 2.5s, giving up 10 minutes past the start.
+
+No Flashbots, no mempool watching, no multi-wallet racing. For a large-supply
+free mint, reacting a couple of seconds after go-live is fine.
+
+Selection rules (`src/drops/parser.ts`, all unit-tested) — every one of these is
+a reason *not* to mint, and anything ambiguous resolves to skip:
+
+| Rule | Behaviour |
+| --- | --- |
+| Chain | Must match `drops.chain` (default `ethereum`). |
+| Stage | Must be a **Public** stage. An active Allowlist/VIP/presale stage is ignored. |
+| Price | Must be `<= drops.maxMintPriceWei` (default `0`). |
+| Unparseable price | Treated as **not free**, never as free. |
+| Supply | Skipped when `totalSupply >= maxSupply`. |
+| Wallet limit | Quantity clamped to `maxPerWallet`; a limit of 0 skips. |
+| Timing | Future stage → watch. Ended stage → skip. |
+| Dedupe | One mint per collection slug, ever. |
+| Retry | One attempt per stage window, so a reverting drop can't loop-spam. |
+
+**Scope boundary:** "live on OpenSea" for *minting* means drops in OpenSea's
+Drops/SeaDrop program — not every ERC-721 with a collection page or secondary
+listings. That's what detector 2 is for.
+
